<p>
As you most of you know, I live a digital life. That doesn&rsquo;t mean I have no existence outside the Internet or that I don&rsquo;t have real life friends and loved ones. It simply means that almost all my transactions and non-face-to-face communications happen digitally. It also means that almost all of my personal information is stored online. As I&rsquo;ve written before, <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=8602">even though we think we&rsquo;re going to live forever, we probably won&rsquo;t</a> so it&rsquo;s important that we arrange to have all that online data available to our survivors.
</p>
<p>
Than can be complicated because there are privacy concerns involved and many online services won&rsquo;t release the data without prior authorization. Of course, it&rsquo;s a hassle to arrange that authorization for all your accounts and you&rsquo;ll probably forget some, even if you try. The better strategy is to provide your survivors with your log on credentials so that they can access whatever data they need.
</p>
<p>
A recent <a href="https://www.sans.org/newsletters/ouch/locked-out-what-happens-your-digital-life-after-death?utm_medium=Email&amp;utm_source=HL-GL&amp;utm_content=1580817_header_image&amp;utm_campaign=OUCH&amp;utm_rdetail=Global&amp;utm_goal=Community_Growth&amp;utm_type=SSA&amp;is=826de51eb0b65e14185bf134b1164621535c59bd1f312917ce85dd036e23c3b1">SANS Ouch! newsletter</a> offers a few suggestions for handling the problem. The most useful, in my opinion is to keep all your logins in a Password Manager and to choose a person to entrust with access to the logins. That&rsquo;s what I&rsquo;ve done and I&rsquo;ve made it an addendum to my will.
</p>
<p>
All this is not something that most of us want to think about but it <i>is</i> important that our families have the necessary data to continue their lives even if that means only having access to pictures of us for remembrance.</p>
